# Copy to .env for local development. In production set these as real
# environment variables in cPanel -> Setup Node.js App -> Environment variables.
DB_HOST=127.0.0.1
DB_PORT=3306
# Set this when MySQL listens on a unix socket rather than on a port, which is
# how shared hosting usually runs it. The giveaway is phpMyAdmin working while
# this app cannot connect at all: cPanel's tools are PHP, and PHP given
# "localhost" uses the socket, where Node's driver dials 127.0.0.1:3306 and is
# refused. Left blank, the well-known paths are tried automatically after a
# refused connection — set it explicitly to skip the guessing.
#   DB_SOCKET=/var/lib/mysql/mysql.sock
DB_SOCKET=
DB_NAME=zimpan
DB_USER=root
DB_PASSWORD=
DB_POOL=5
PORT=3000
# NODE_ENV=production   # enables Secure cookies and trust proxy

# Google sign-in. Leave blank to disable it entirely: the button will not
# render and /api/auth/google will refuse. Create the client at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials (Web application).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# Nutrition estimates from Claude. Leave blank to disable: the "Refine with AI"
# button will not render and /api/estimate will refuse, so the app runs exactly
# as before on the local estimate alone. Key from https://console.anthropic.com/
# Costs money per estimate — it is only ever called when a user asks for one.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# Optional. Model names change; override if the default stops being available,
# or to trade accuracy for cost: claude-sonnet-5 is roughly half the price of
# claude-opus-5, claude-haiku-4-5 roughly a fifth. Switching is env-only —
# the effort level is dropped automatically for models that reject it.
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# Optional. low (default) | medium | high | off. Only sent to models that
# accept it; "off" forces it out for a model this code has not heard of.
ANTHROPIC_EFFORT=low

# ── Outbound email ──
#
# Two things depend on this: password resets, and team invitations. With
# SMTP_HOST or MAIL_FROM blank the mailer stays off — outside production it
# prints links to the console so the flows can be exercised locally, and in
# production it refuses to send rather than writing them into a log.
#
# Nothing else breaks when it is off, which is exactly why it is easy to leave
# unset and never notice: an invitation is still created and the admin is shown
# a link to pass on by hand. The screen now says which of the two happened.
#
# On cPanel, create a mailbox (Email Accounts) and use it as the sender. The
# loopback MTA is the simplest route — it never leaves the machine, so no
# password is needed and the certificate mismatch on "localhost" is tolerated:
#   SMTP_HOST=localhost
#   SMTP_PORT=25
#   MAIL_FROM=ZIMPAN <no-reply@zimpan.com>
# Or authenticate against the mail server proper, which is what a mailbox's
# own settings page will tell you to use:
#   SMTP_HOST=mail.zimpan.com
#   SMTP_PORT=465
#   SMTP_SECURE=true
#   SMTP_USER=no-reply@zimpan.com
#   SMTP_PASSWORD=…
#
# MAIL_FROM must be a real mailbox on the sending domain. A From address the
# domain has not authorised is what SPF and DMARC exist to reject, and mail
# that is rejected for that reason usually disappears without a bounce.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_SECURE=false
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=

# Where Help requests from the app's footer are sent. The sender's own address
# is set as Reply-To, so answering the notification replies to them directly.
SUPPORT_EMAIL=admin@bigcavestudios.com

# ── Reminders (Web Push) ──
#
# One notification a day per browser, at an hour the person chose, about what
# that day holds. Off entirely until VAPID_PUBLIC and VAPID_PRIVATE are set —
# with them blank the app never offers the switch, which is the correct
# behaviour for an install that has not set this up.
#
# Make the pair once, on the server:
#
#   cd ~/zimpan/server && node push-keys.js
#
# If that says "node: command not found", node is inside the application's
# virtual environment rather than on the PATH — run the `source .../bin/activate`
# line from cPanel's Setup Node.js App screen first. docs/reminders.md also has
# an openssl recipe that needs no node at all.
#
# Paste both lines here (or into cPanel → Setup Node.js App → Environment
# variables) and restart. Generating a second pair silently unsubscribes
# everybody: a browser's subscription is bound to the key it subscribed with,
# and there is no way to move one. So make them once and keep them.
#
# The private half signs; anybody holding it can send notifications as this
# app. It belongs here and nowhere else — not in the repository, not in a log.
VAPID_PUBLIC=
VAPID_PRIVATE=
# Where a push service should complain. Must be a mailto: or an https: URL;
# Google and Mozilla both reject a token without one.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@bigcavestudios.com

# Nothing is sent until something asks for a round. In cPanel → Cron Jobs, add
# one every ten minutes (*/10 * * * *):
#
#   curl -fsS -m 60 -X POST -H "X-Zimpan-Cron: <the value below>" \
#     https://www.zimpan.com/api/push/run >/dev/null
#
# The round works out what time it is on each device and sends to the ones
# whose chosen minute has just passed, so the cron's interval is the only
# thing that decides how late a reminder can be. Every ten minutes is well
# inside what anybody notices; hourly would mean an 8am reminder arriving at
# some point before nine.
#
# Make this a long random string. With it unset the endpoint answers 404 to
# everybody, including the cron.
PUSH_CRON_SECRET=

# How late a reminder may be and still be worth sending, in minutes. A server
# that was down all morning should be on time tomorrow rather than greeting
# everybody at noon with a reminder about a morning that has gone.
PUSH_LATE_MIN=120
